A syntax error within the expression of a template literal placeholder
made the compiler spin until it ran out of memory. The statement
terminating the placeholder expression had advanced the source position
beyond the last token of the expression, and instructions emitted for
that token afterwards moved the position backwards. The negative offset
was taken for an enormous one when recording the source position of the
instruction, which then took practically forever and a lot of memory.

1. Excess token in a placeholder.

-- Testcase --
x = `a${ 1 2 }`;
-- End --

-- Args --
-R
-- End --

-- Expect stderr --
Syntax error: Unexpected token
Expecting '}'
In line 1, byte 12:

 `x = `a${ 1 2 }`;`
  ~~~~~~~~~~~^


-- End --


2. Unterminated placeholder at the end of the source.

-- Testcase --
x = `tpl${
-- End --

-- Args --
-R
-- End --

-- Expect stderr --
Syntax error: Expecting expression
In line 2, byte 1:

 `x = `tpl${`
            ^-- Near here


-- End --
